{"causes": ["The ongoing ideological and political conflict between the Holy Roman Emperors and the Papacy in the 13th and 14th centuries necessitated strong intellectual defenses of papal power.", "The flourishing of scholastic philosophy, particularly the political thought of Thomas Aquinas, provided a foundational framework that Bartholomew sought to expand and apply.", "The need for comprehensive historical narratives from a pro-papal perspective during a period of intense ecclesiastical and political upheaval spurred his extensive historiographical work."], "effects": ["Bartholomew's completion of *De Regimine Principum* significantly shaped medieval political theory by asserting the Pope's ultimate temporal authority over secular rulers.", "His *Historia Ecclesiastica Nova* provided a foundational, pro-papal historical narrative that influenced subsequent generations of ecclesiastical historians and apologists.", "He contributed to the transmission and interpretation of Thomistic political philosophy, extending its application to contemporary debates on church-state relations."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "thomas-aquinas", "sourceName": "Thomas Aquinas", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "bartholomew-of-lucca", "targetName": "Bartholomew of Lucca", "context": "Thomas Aquinas's unfinished *De Regimine Principum* provided the foundational text that Bartholomew of Lucca completed, extending its arguments on kingship."}, {"sourceSlug": "bartholomew-of-lucca", "sourceName": "Bartholomew of Lucca", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "papal-supremacy", "targetName": "Papal Supremacy", "context": "Bartholomew's historical and theoretical writings consistently advocated for the supremacy of papal authority over temporal rulers."}, {"sourceSlug": "guelphs-and-ghibellines", "sourceName": "Guelphs and Ghibellines", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "bartholomew-of-lucca", "targetName": "Bartholomew of Lucca", "context": "The intense political rivalry between the Guelphs (pro-papal) and Ghibellines (pro-imperial) shaped Bartholomew's strong pro-papal stance and writings."}, {"sourceSlug": "bartholomew-of-lucca", "sourceName": "Bartholomew of Lucca", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "lucca-italy", "targetName": "Lucca, Italy", "context": "Bartholomew served as prior of the Dominican convent in Lucca, where he undertook significant portions of his scholarly work."}, {"sourceSlug": "bartholomew-of-lucca", "sourceName": "Bartholomew of Lucca", "verb": "CREATES", "targetSlug": "historia-ecclesiastica-nova", "targetName": "Historia Ecclesiastica Nova", "context": "He authored the *Historia Ecclesiastica Nova*, a comprehensive history from creation to 1312, offering a distinct theological and pro-papal perspective."}], "places": [{"name": "Lucca, Italy", "role": "Dominican Priorate"}, {"name": "Torcello, Italy", "role": "Bishopric"}, {"name": "Avignon, France", "role": "Papal Court Connection"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Bartholomew of Lucca", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "bartholomew-of-lucca"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q3410332", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ptolemy_of_Lucca"], "tags": ["Journalists & Chroniclers", "cause-and-effect", "cultural-diffusion", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Bartholomew of Lucca significantly shaped medieval political and ecclesiastical thought by completing Aquinas's *De Regimine Principum* to advocate for papal temporal supremacy and by providing a comprehensive pro-papal historical narrative in his *Historia Ecclesiastica Nova*, influencing subsequent church-state debates.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-08-07T12:26:48Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#31177778871", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Tolomeo Fiadoni was an Italian historian and political theorist.", "newValue": "Bartholomew of Lucca, known as Tolomeo Fiadoni (died 1327), was a pivotal Italian Dominican friar, historian, and political theorist whose extensive writings provided a staunch defense of papal supremacy during the turbulent late medieval period. Operating in an era marked by intense conflicts between the papacy and secular rulers, particularly the Holy Roman Empire, Bartholomew’s intellectual contributions spanned from completing the seminal political treatise *De Regimine Principum* begun by Thomas Aquinas, to authoring comprehensive ecclesiastical histories that shaped subsequent understandings of papal authority.

His most significant historical works include the *Historia Ecclesiastica Nova*, a vast chronicle extending from creation to 1312, which served as a crucial source for later historians, and the *Annales*, covering the years 1061 to 1303. As a friar, and later Bishop of Torcello, Bartholomew was deeply embedded in the intellectual and political currents of his time, often writing from a distinctly Guelph, pro-papal perspective. His completion of Aquinas’s *De Regimine Principum* (Books II-IV), undertaken between 1301 and 1303, extended the discourse on ideal kingship to explicitly assert the pope's ultimate temporal power, thereby transforming a work on political ethics into a potent instrument for papal claims.

Bartholomew's legacy lies in his robust articulation and historical substantiation of papal temporal power, which profoundly influenced later canon lawyers and political thinkers who grappled with the relationship between church and state. His *Historia Ecclesiastica Nova* became a standard reference for the history of the papacy, transmitting a particular narrative of ecclesiastical authority through centuries. By systematically documenting papal actions and arguments, he provided a historical and theological framework that reinforced the 'regnum et sacerdotium' doctrine, shaping the intellectual battlegrounds of the 14th and 15th centuries."}, {"timestamp": "2026-08-07T12:26:48Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#31177778871", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Medieval era: interplay of religious authority, feudal governance, and trade network expansion", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "1240, Lucca"}, {"title": "Rise of print media and public discourse", "type": "Idea", "year": "1240, Lucca"}], "newValue": ["The ongoing ideological and political conflict between the Holy Roman Emperors and the Papacy in the 13th and 14th centuries necessitated strong intellectual defenses of papal power.", "The flourishing of scholastic philosophy, particularly the political thought of Thomas Aquinas, provided a foundational framework that Bartholomew sought to expand and apply.", "The need for comprehensive historical narratives from a pro-papal perspective during a period of intense ecclesiastical and political upheaval spurred his extensive historiographical work."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-08-07T12:26:48Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#31177778871", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Shaped public opinion and historical record", "type": "Idea", "year": "1327"}, {"title": "Contributed to synthesis of classical and religious thought informing Renaissance and Reformati", "type": "Movement", "year": "1327"}], "newValue": ["Bar